# Implement to the industry bar

Read the rule and the component that already own the surface. Ship that pattern. A feature-only class, a second copy of a list, or a padding number invented for one screen is a rejected patch — remove it in the same change, before calling the work done.

## Bar

- **One layout.** Compose the component that already paints the settled screen. Do not fork its markup.
- **Stable first paint.** The first frame is the final layout. A stand-in that then shifts (Cumulative Layout Shift) is a failed open.
- **Tokens.** Spacing, type, and color come from the existing chrome tokens (`--os-screen-body-pad-x`, sheet heights, the face rule). Do not invent a one-screen number.
- **Cascade.** A selector that loses in the browser does not exist. Match or beat the rule you must override, then confirm the computed value.
- **Dismiss stays reachable.** Covering a surface keeps a labeled close and the drag handle. Do not trap the page.
- **Same-change cleanup.** Delete what this change orphaned: unused exports, duplicate markup, CSS for a class nothing renders, helpers with no callers.
- **Locked decisions.** When the user changes a product rule, update that rule in the same change.

## Do not

- Mount a stand-in page and restyle it until the real page arrives.
- Leave the rejected approach in the tree because the new path works.
- Wait to be asked to clean leftovers.

```tsx
// Rejected: a second card tree, then a padding rule only that tree uses.
<ul className="portfolio-writing-list">...</ul>

// The shelf already has the inset. Render it.
<PortfolioWritingList ... />
```

```css
/* Rejected: loses to the 720px drawer cap, so the sheet never grows. */
.glass-sheet-panel.page-drawer-panel { height: 95dvh; }

/* Wins: same weight as the cap, later in the app sheet. */
.glass-sheet-root .glass-sheet-panel.page-drawer-panel { height: 100%; }
```
